<Docs
  title="Running GitWarren on Linux — AppImage, headless daemon, systemd"
  description="Install the GitWarren AppImage on a Linux desktop, or run the headless daemon on a server with no screen and review it from somewhere else."
  eyebrow="Linux"
  heading="A desktop, or a box with no screen."
  intro="Linux is where GitWarren's two shells matter most, because Linux is where half the machines have no screen. A workstation gets the app; a server gets the daemon and is reviewed from the laptop you are actually sitting at."
>
  <h2>On a desktop</h2>

  <p>
    Download the AppImage for your architecture — x86_64 or arm64 — from{" "}
    <a href="/#download">the front page</a>, make it executable, and run it.
  </p>

  <pre><code>chmod +x GitWarren-*.AppImage
./GitWarren-*.AppImage</code></pre>

  <p>
    There is deliberately no <code>.deb</code> and no <code>.rpm</code>. Neither
    can update itself without a package manager and a sudo prompt, and silent
    background updates are worth more here than distribution packaging is.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="note-title">No system tray? Still fine</span>
    <p>
      GitWarren wants to live in the tray, and plenty of Linux desktops do not
      have one. Where there is no tray to put an icon in, you get an ordinary
      window instead of an app that starts and appears to do nothing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>On a server</h2>

  <p>
    A machine with no screen runs the command-line half: the same daemon, the
    same MCP server, no Electron.
  </p>

  <pre><code><span class="c"># brings its own Node</span>
brew install klarluft/tap/gitwarren-cli

<span class="c"># or use the Node already on the box</span>
npx gitwarren serve</code></pre>

  <p>
    You usually do not have to do even that. If you can reach the box over ssh
    from a machine running the app, <a href="/docs/another-machine"
      >add it as a host</a
    > and GitWarren installs itself there over the same connection — the host
    needs nothing but git.
  </p>

  <h3>Starting at boot</h3>

  <p>
    <code>gitwarren service install</code> writes the{" "}
    <code>~/.gitwarren/bin/</code> launchers and registers a{" "}
    <code>systemd --user</code> unit that runs{" "}
    <code>gitwarren serve --listen</code> at login.
  </p>

  <pre><code>gitwarren service install
gitwarren service status

<span class="c"># launchers only, no login item — what a host reached over ssh wants</span>
gitwarren service install --no-login-item</code></pre>

  <p>
    Nothing restarts a dead daemon, and that is deliberate rather than an
    oversight. <code>serve --listen</code> is <em>meant</em> to exit when the
    desktop app is already running — a data directory has one owner — and under
    a <code>Restart=</code> policy that refusal would become a process
    respawning every ten seconds for as long as GitWarren is open.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <code>systemd --user</code> unit stops when you log out unless lingering is
    enabled for the account. On a server you want{" "}
    <code>loginctl enable-linger $USER</code>, which is a systemd setting rather
    than anything GitWarren does.
  </p>

  <h2>On your tailnet</h2>

  <p>
    Linux needs one permission the other platforms do not.{" "}
    <code>tailscale serve</code> refuses to run without root until it has been
    told your user may drive it:
  </p>

  <pre><code>sudo tailscale set --operator=$USER</code></pre>

  <p>
    Once, per machine. Then <strong>Reachable on your tailnet</strong> works as
    it does everywhere else — see <a href="/docs/tailnet">the tailnet guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>git, and nothing else</h2>

  <p>
    GitWarren shells out to your own <code>git</code> rather than bundling an
    implementation, so git has to be installed and on the <code>PATH</code>. If
    it is not, the app says so explicitly instead of showing you an empty list.
  </p>
</Docs>
